diagnosis_text:
A thorough multi-layer investigation found no network fault. The WiFi segment (VLAN 40, 10.6.0.0/24) and its VRRP gateway are functioning correctly. dswa2 holds the 10.6.0.1 VIP on br0.40, consistent with its keepalived configuration (VI_40: state MASTER, priority 105). laptop1 has a valid IP (10.6.0.100/24), a correct default route via 10.6.0.1, and achieves 0% loss to every internal subnet tested — its local gateway, pc1 (10.1.0.100 on VLAN 10), pc3 (10.3.0.100 on the B-wing VLAN), the DNS server srv1 (10.5.0.4), and all VRRP VIPs across the topology. The OSPF fabric is healthy on all core and distribution routers (32 routes each). No ACLs block laptop1's traffic paths, no MAC conflicts exist, and pressure_sweep found no resource stress on any device. The two apparent anomalies — DNS REFUSED for external names and inability to ping 8.8.8.8 — are not faults. The named.conf on srv1 explicitly sets `recursion no` with an inline comment documenting this as intentional for an offline lab with no upstream resolver; srv1 correctly serves the local jeremysitlab.com zone (including the wildcard record) and returns authoritative answers for all internal names. The 8.8.8.8 failure is equally expected: the lab uses simulated ISP addresses (203.0.113.0/24) without real internet forwarding, and pc1 shows the identical failure. The team's VRRP hypothesis is ruled out by direct evidence on dswa2. The user's complaint is not corroborated by any measurable network failure.
